---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CalcMoneyMarginMarkup from '@/components/tools/finance/CalcMoneyMarginMarkup.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="profit-margin-calculator">
  <CalcMoneyMarginMarkup client:load variant="margin" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>The margin formula</h2>
    <pre><code>gross profit  = selling price − cost
gross margin % = gross profit ÷ selling price × 100</code></pre>
    <p>
      The denominator is the <strong>selling price</strong>. That is the whole definition, and it is
      what separates margin from markup: margin asks "what fraction of the money I took did I keep?",
      markup asks "how much did I add to what I paid?"
    </p>
    <p>
      Sell for 100 after you paid 62, and you keep 38 of each 100 that comes through the till: a 38%
      gross margin. To go the other way and price for a target margin, divide the cost by one minus
      the margin: 62 ÷ 0.62 = 100.
    </p>

    <h2>Gross, operating and net margin</h2>
    <p>
      "Profit margin" means three different things depending on how far down the income statement you
      are. Only the first is a per-product number, and only the first is what this page computes.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[520px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Margin</th>
            <th>Revenue minus…</th>
            <th>Used for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Gross</strong></td>
            <td>direct cost of goods sold</td>
            <td>Pricing a product, comparing lines</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Operating</strong></td>
            <td>COGS plus operating expenses</td>
            <td>Judging how efficiently the business runs</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Net</strong></td>
            <td>everything, including interest and tax</td>
            <td>What the owners actually keep</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      A shop can run a 55% gross margin and a 2% net margin quite comfortably. The gap is rent, wages
      and everything else that does not vary with the individual sale.
    </p>

    <h2>What counts as cost of goods</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>In: the item itself, inbound freight, import duty, packaging, per-unit manufacturing labour.</li>
      <li>Usually in for e-commerce: payment-processing fees and marketplace commission, because they scale exactly with each sale.</li>
      <li>Out: rent, salaried staff, software subscriptions, marketing, insurance. These are operating expenses and belong below the gross line.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Be consistent. Whether you put card fees in COGS matters far less than whether you put them in
      the same place every month, because margin is only useful as a trend.
    </p>

    <h2>Typical gross margins by sector</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[380px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Sector</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Typical gross margin</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>Grocery retail</td><td style="text-align:right">20–28%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>General merchandise retail</td><td style="text-align:right">30–45%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Restaurant food</td><td style="text-align:right">60–70%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Restaurant drinks</td><td style="text-align:right">70–80%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Apparel and accessories</td><td style="text-align:right">50–65%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Professional services</td><td style="text-align:right">50–70%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Software (SaaS)</td><td style="text-align:right">70–85%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm">
      Broad industry ranges for orientation only, not a benchmark to be measured against. The
      comparison that matters is your own margin last quarter.
    </p>

    <h2>Why a small margin change matters more than a small volume change</h2>
    <p>
      Gross profit changes linearly with volume and with margin, but a price rise carries no
      extra cost, while extra volume does. Raising prices 5% on a 30% margin lifts gross profit by
      about 17%, because the whole increase is profit. Selling 5% more units lifts it by 5%. That
      asymmetry is why margin is where operators look first.
    </p>
    <p>
      The reverse is equally sharp: a 5% discount off a 30% margin gives away a sixth of the profit on
      every unit sold, and you need roughly 20% more volume to stand still.
    </p>

    <h2>Margin, discounts and tax</h2>
    <p>
      Two rules keep margin correct. Calculate it on the net price, before VAT, GST or sales tax,
      because that money was never yours. And calculate it on the actual price
      after discounts, not the list price, because a 20%-off promotion on a 40% margin item leaves a
      25% margin, and averaging a promotional month against list prices hides exactly the problem you
      are looking for.
    </p>

    <h2>Runs entirely in your browser</h2>
    <p>
      Cost prices and margins are competitive information. This page never sends them anywhere: there
      is no server, no analytics and no third-party request, and the bulk pricing table and its CSV,
      Excel and PDF exports are all produced locally. It works offline.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
